{"causes":["The establishment of the Roman Republic in 509 BC created a power vacuum that would eventually lead to the rise of Augustus and the end of the Roman Republic.","The Punic Wars with Carthage, fought between 264 and 146 BC, marked a turning point in Roman history as the empire expanded its territories and established itself as a dominant world power.","The construction of the Appian Way in 312 BC facilitated trade, commerce, and communication throughout the Roman Empire."],"effects":["The spread of Latin and the adoption of Roman law had a profound impact on the development of modern languages and legal systems.","The rise of Christianity under Emperor Constantine in 313 AD led to the eventual decline of paganism and the establishment of Christianity as a dominant world religion.","The Roman Empire's extensive infrastructure, including roads, aqueducts, and public buildings, provided a model for urban planning and development that would influence Western cities for centuries to come."],"relationships":[{"sourceSlug":"titus-livius","sourceName":"Titus Livius","verb":"CREATES","targetSlug":"history-of-rome","targetName":"History of Rome","context":"The History of Rome was written by Titus Livius, a Roman historian and politician."},{"sourceSlug":"roman-republic","sourceName":"Roman Republic","verb":"OCCURS_DURING","targetSlug":"history-of-rome","targetName":"History of Rome","context":"The History of Rome chronicles the rise and fall of the Roman Empire, which began during the Roman Republic."},{"sourceSlug":"augustus","sourceName":"Augustus","verb":"SUCCEEDS","targetSlug":"history-of-rome","targetName":"History of Rome","context":"The History of Rome describes the transformation of the Roman Republic into an empire under the rule of Augustus."},{"sourceSlug":"punic-wars","sourceName":"Punic Wars","verb":"OCCURS_IN","targetSlug":"italy","targetName":"Italy","context":"The Punic Wars were fought in various locations across Italy, including the Mediterranean coastline and the Italian peninsula."},{"sourceSlug":"colosseum","sourceName":"Colosseum","verb":"CONTAINS","targetSlug":"rome","targetName":"Rome","context":"The Colosseum was built in Rome during the reign of Emperor Vespasian."},{"sourceSlug":"latin-language","sourceName":"Latin Language","verb":"TRANSMITS","targetSlug":"western-civilization","targetName":"Western Civilization","context":"The spread of Latin had a profound impact on the development of modern languages and cultural identity."},{"sourceSlug":"christianity","sourceName":"Christianity","verb":"OCCURS_DURING","targetSlug":"roman-empire","targetName":"Roman Empire","context":"The rise of Christianity under Emperor Constantine marked a significant turning point in the history of the Roman Empire."},{"sourceSlug":"augustus-law","sourceName":"Augustus Law","verb":"DEFINES","targetSlug":"roman-law","targetName":"Roman Law","context":"The Augustus law established a model for civil governance and justice that would influence the development of Roman law."}],"places":[{"name":"Rome, Italy","role":"Capital"},{"name":"Appian Way, Italy","role":"Road"},{"name":"Mediterranean coastline, Europe","role":"Battlefield"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: History of Rome","type":"Reference article","slug":"history-of-rome"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q1155892","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_Rome_(Livy)"],"tags":["literary work","Histories & Chronicles","cause-and-effect"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"'This is the most wholesome and beneficial thing about history: you see examples of every kind of behaviour set forth on a clear and illuminated monument, and from that you can take both what to imitate and what to avoid.' — Livy, Preface to Ab Urbe Condita","legacySummary":"History of Rome contributed to the historical record of the Classical period. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.","summary":"Ab Urbe Condita (Latin: 'From the Founding of the City') is a monumental history of Rome from its mythological founding by Romulus and Remus in 753 BCE to the reign of Augustus, composed by the Roman historian Titus Livius — Livy (c. 64 BCE–17 CE) — in 142 books, of which 35 survive complete (Books 1–10 covering 753–293 BCE and Books 21–45 covering 218–167 BCE). Livy worked in Rome under the patronage of Augustus Caesar, who reportedly called him 'a Pompeian' despite his republican sympathies — a remark that testifies to the independence and seriousness of Livy's engagement with the Roman past. His History combines rhetorical skill, patriotic sentiment, moral purpose, and detailed narrative in a prose style that was admired throughout antiquity as a model of Latin eloquence.

Ab Urbe Condita is the primary source for many of the most celebrated episodes of early and middle Roman history — the Rape of Lucretia and the founding of the Roman Republic (509 BCE), the Gallic sack of Rome (390 BCE), the Samnite Wars, and above all the Second Punic War (218–202 BCE) — Hannibal's crossing of the Alps, the catastrophic Roman defeat at Cannae (216 BCE) where approximately 70,000 Roman soldiers died in a single afternoon, and the dramatic rise of Scipio Africanus and Rome's ultimate victory. Though Livy's method was more rhetorical than strictly critical — he reproduced and embellished earlier Roman annalists and legendary traditions rather than subjecting sources to rigorous scrutiny — his depictions of Roman heroes (Horatius Cocles at the bridge, Cincinnatus called from his plough, Fabius Maximus the Delayer, Scipio Africanus) became the canonical images of Roman virtue in Western education for two millennia.

Livy's History was the foundational text of Roman history in Western education from the imperial period through the medieval manuscript tradition to the Renaissance humanists who saw Republican Rome as the supreme model of civic virtue. Machiavelli's Discourses on Livy (c. 1517) — his analysis of republican government, civic virtue, and political realism — is the most influential political text of the Renaissance, treating Livy's Rome as a case study in political theory that remains applicable to all times. The French and American Revolutionaries drew on Livy's Roman exempla as political models, and the Roman republican vocabulary that pervades the US founding documents — Senate, Capitol, consul, republic, dictator — is mediated substantially through the Livian tradition. Only 35 of the original 142 books survive; the losses represent some of the most lamented gaps in classical scholarship.","historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":"The History of Rome had a profound impact on Western civilization, shaping the course of politics, law, architecture, and art.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[]}
